VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-044
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of sharp, sudden anterior chest pain and severe shortness of breath during mild physical exertion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a young, exceptionally tall adolescent who suddenly developed severe, sharp, one-sided chest pain and profound shortness of breath after simply reaching for an object. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was critically required to definitively rule out a spontaneous pneumothorax (highly prevalent in this specific body habitus) or an acute aortic dissection (in the context of suspected Marfan syndrome), prior to the availability of the regular GP.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I just reached up to grab a book and I felt a sharp tearing pain in the right side of my chest, and now I can't catch my breath at all."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a hyperacute onset of sharp, pleuritic right-sided chest pain and sudden severe dyspnoea. The underlying chronic history is highly significant: the patient has a standing height more than two standard deviations above the mean for age and sex, with an accelerated linear growth velocity. The parent notes a distinct Marfanoid habitus, characterized by excessively long, thin extremities and arachnodactyly (unusually long digits). The patient has a known pectus excavatum chest wall deformity and profound joint hypermobility. The acute event occurred precisely 30 minutes ago without any significant blunt trauma. The pain is severely exacerbated by deep inspiration.
Functional Status: Sitting rigidly upright, gasping for breath, unable to speak in full sentences.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme risk of rapid respiratory or cardiovascular collapse; severe acute physical distress.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/65 | HR: 118 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 28 | SpO2: 91% (Room Air) | Wt: 70 Kg (Height: 198cm)
General: Patient appears pale, anxious, tachypnoeic, and is visibly splinting the right side of the chest during respiration.
Physical Exam: Physical inspection confirms a profound Marfanoid habitus (tall stature, arachnodactyly, pectus excavatum, positive wrist/thumb signs). Respiratory examination reveals a hyper-resonant percussion note over the right upper and middle lung fields. Crucially, auscultation demonstrates completely absent breath sounds over the entire right hemithorax, with normal vesicular sounds on the left. Trachea remains midline (no current tension). Cardiovascular exam reveals sinus tachycardia, with a soft, early diastolic murmur noted at the right upper sternal border (suspicious for aortic root dilation/regurgitation). The presentation firmly establishes the context of tall stature (>2 SD, accelerated growth) and immediately confirms a secondary acute respiratory emergency.
Environmental Observations: Patient is seated in a chair, leaning forward; the environment is clear for stretcher access.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Spontaneous Right-Sided Pneumothorax secondary to underlying Connective Tissue Disorder (Suspected Marfan Syndrome) - Status: Medical Emergency
Differentials:

Acute Aortic Dissection - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary cause of the absent breath sounds, though the patient's habitus places them at extreme risk, and the sudden sharp pain mandates urgent CT angiography alongside chest radiography in the ED.

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to the unilateral hyper-resonance and entirely absent breath sounds, which are pathognomonic for a collapsed lung rather than a vascular occlusion.

Musculoskeletal Strain - Ruled out/unlikely due to the objective finding of severe hypoxia (SpO2 91%) and completely absent unilateral breath sounds.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (Medical Risk: Extreme - risk of tension pneumothorax)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with classic signs of an acute spontaneous pneumothorax (sudden pleuritic pain, hypoxia, absent unilateral breath sounds) occurring in the textbook demographic: a young, exceptionally tall male with a Marfanoid habitus. The rapid growth and connective tissue abnormalities lead to the formation of fragile apical pulmonary blebs that rupture easily. The presence of hypoxia and significant tachycardia indicates a large, clinically significant pneumothorax. Deferring care was categorically impossible. The immediate after-hours home assessment confirmed the absence of a tension pneumothorax (trachea midline, BP stable) but mandated an immediate transfer to the Emergency Department for urgent chest radiography and likely tube thoracostomy (chest drain) insertion.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate escalation to the Emergency Department via ambulance (000 called by clinician). Administered high-flow oxygen (15L/min via non-rebreather mask) from the clinical response bag to treat acute hypoxia and accelerate pleural nitrogen washout. Advised the receiving ED of the suspected large spontaneous pneumothorax in a patient with suspected Marfan syndrome, requesting urgent surgical review and echocardiography to assess the aortic root.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent the collapsed lung from converting into a fatal tension pneumothorax, remain sitting strictly upright and avoid any deep coughing or bearing down, directly minimizing the pressure buildup inside the chest cavity. To maximize oxygen delivery to the healthy lung, keep the provided oxygen mask sealed tightly over your face until the paramedics arrive.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None. Immediate ED transfer.
DME/Safety (Optional): Oxygen therapy initiated.
Follow-up: Post-discharge multi-disciplinary review involving GP, respiratory physician, and clinical geneticist for formal Marfan syndrome diagnosis and advanced bone age/IGF-1 workup.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance is en route. Patient is continuously monitored by the clinician for signs of tension (e.g., sudden drop in blood pressure, distended neck veins, severe worsening of shortness of breath).

