Disclaimer: The physical examination confirms a collapsed lung requiring immediate hospital treatment; your underlying body type makes you prone to this.
Clinical Course: The hospital team will likely need to insert a small tube into your chest to suck the trapped air out and allow the lung to re-inflate; you will remain in the hospital for several days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to stand or walk. Keep the oxygen mask on. Inform me immediately if you feel a sudden, extreme worsening of pressure in your chest or if you feel faint.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Because you have grown so tall and so rapidly, the tissue at the top of your lungs is stretched and fragile. A small blister on the lung surface has popped, allowing air to leak into the chest cavity and crush the lung, which is why I cannot hear any breath sounds on your right side. The hospital will insert a tube to remove this trapped air.
Actionable Steps: Stay sitting upright, as this allows gravity to help your remaining good lung work effectively. Do not take off the oxygen mask, as it is treating your low blood oxygen levels.

